draw(Circle((9,9),9),linewidth(0.7));
draw((1,3)--(1,4),dashed);
draw((9,9)--(9,0),dashed);
label("$r$",(9,4.5),E);
draw((9,9)--(1,3),linewidth(0.7));
label("$r+s$",(5,6),SE);
draw((1,3)--(1,9)--(9,9));
label("$r-s$",(5,9),N);
draw((1,8)--(2,8)--(2,9));
draw((-0.5,0)--(-1,0)--(-1,9)--(-0.5,9));
draw((-0.5,3)--(-1,3));
draw((0,-0.5)--(0,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,-0.5));
label("$r-3s$",(-1,6),W);
label("$3s$",(-1,1.5),W);
label("$s$",(0.5,-1),S);
dot((1,1));
dot((3,1));
dot((1,3));

[/asy]

Consider a right triangle as shown.  Applying the Pythagorean theorem yields \[(r+s)^2=(r-3s)^2+(r-s)^2 \]Simplifying,  \begin{align*}
r^2+2rs+s^2&=r^2-6rs+9s^2+r^2-2rs+s^2\\
0&=r^2-10rs+9s^2\\
&=(r-9s)(r-s)\\
\end{align*}But we know that $r\neq s$, so the only solution is $r = 9s$; hence $r/s = \boxed{9}.$

